The '''2011 FIFA Club World Cup''' (officially known as the '''FIFA Club World Cup Japan 2011 presented by Toyota''' for sponsorship reasons) was a [[association football|football]] tournament that was played from 8 to 18 December 2011.<ref>{{cite web |title=Match Schedule – FIFA Club World Cup Japan 2011 |url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/01/46/40/33/fcwc2011_matchschedule_05122011.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120131105201/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/tournament/competition/01/46/40/33/fcwc2011_matchschedule_05122011.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=31 January 2012 |publisher=FIFA |date=5 December 2011 |access-date=2 August 2012 }}</ref> It was the eighth edition of the [[FIFA Club World Cup]], a [[FIFA]]-organised tournament between the winners of the six continental confederations as well as the host nation's league champions.

After the [[United Arab Emirates]] hosted the tournament in [[2009 FIFA Club World Cup|2009]] and [[2010 FIFA Club World Cup|2010]], hosting rights for the 2011 edition returned to Japan.<ref name="host">{{cite news|title=Unanimous support for 6+5, FIFA Club World Cup hosts revealed |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/organisation/media/newsid=779200.html |publisher=FIFA |date=27 May 2008 |access-date=1 April 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091014213232/http://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/organisation/media/newsid%3D779200.html |archive-date=14 October 2009 }}</ref><ref name="ESPN">{{cite news |title=FIFA moves Club World Cup to UAE from Japan |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=539272&cc=5739 |work=ESPN Soccernet |date=27 May 2008 |access-date=2 April 2010 |archive-date=8 November 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121108105217/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/news/story?id=539272 |url-status=dead }}</ref> During a visit to Japan on 23 May 2011, [[FIFA]] President [[Sepp Blatter]] confirmed that Japan would remain as hosts of the tournament despite the [[2011 Tōhoku earthquake and tsunami]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Blatter reveals double boost for Japan |url=https://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/newsid=1439518/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110808112439/http://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/newsid=1439518/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=8 August 2011 |publisher=FIFA |date=23 May 2011 |access-date=2 August 2012 }}</ref>

Defending champions [[Inter Milan|Internazionale]] did not qualify as they were eliminated in the quarter-finals of the [[2010–11 UEFA Champions League]]. The eventual winners of that competition, Spanish club [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]], went on to win the Club World Cup, winning 4–0 in the semi-finals against Qatari club [[Al Sadd SC|Al-Sadd]] before another victory by the same margin against Brazilian club [[Santos FC|Santos]] in the [[2011 FIFA Club World Cup Final|final]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Magic Messi helps Barca conquer the world |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=334217&cc=5739 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120111215729/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=334217&cc=5739 |url-status=dead |archive-date=11 January 2012 |work=ESPN Soccernet |date=18 December 2011 |access-date=21 December 2011 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Barcelona 4 Santos 0 |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/premier-league/8964048/Barcelona-4-Santos-0-match-report.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120110081459/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/premier-league/8964048/Barcelona-4-Santos-0-match-report.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=10 January 2012 |work=The Daily Telegraph|date=18 December 2011 |access-date=21 December 2011 }}</ref>

